WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.287
Welcome to week five.

2
00:00:01.337 --> 00:00:04.965
The theme is that autonomy is a permission, not a feature.

3
00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:07.306
This week you attack your own agent.

4
00:00:07.356 --> 00:00:17.672
The first video is about the attack that matters most for agents, prompt injection, and a simple way to tell whether your design is exposed to the worst version of it.

5
00:00:18.422 --> 00:00:20.686
Prompt injection comes in two forms.

6
00:00:20.736 --> 00:00:27.240
Direct injection is a user typing instructions that override yours: ignore your rules and do this instead.

7
00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:31.096
It is annoying and it is mostly a reputational problem.

8
00:00:31.146 --> 00:00:32.639
Indirect injection is different.

9
00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:39.579
The instructions arrive inside data the agent reads: an email, a web page, a document, a tool result.

10
00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:46.134
The model reads them as part of its context, and models cannot reliably tell data from instructions.

11
00:00:46.184 --> 00:00:49.990
Nobody has found a way to make them do so.

12
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:57.316
That is the form that matters for agents, because agents read untrusted data all day, and they have tools.

13
00:00:58.066 --> 00:01:03.131
Here is what it looks like in a product you may use.

14
00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:08.247
In June 2025 researchers disclosed EchoLeak, a vulnerability in Microsoft 365 Copilot.

15
00:01:08.297 --> 00:01:12.510
One crafted email, sitting in the inbox, contained hidden instructions.

16
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:27.431
When the user later asked Copilot an ordinary question, Copilot pulled the email into its context, followed the instructions, gathered sensitive content from the user's files and mail, and sent it out through a link.

17
00:01:27.481 --> 00:01:29.136
The user clicked nothing.

18
00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:34.252
Microsoft fixed it before it was exploited, as far as anyone knows.

19
00:01:34.302 --> 00:01:36.810
The design lesson survives the fix.

20
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.550
Simon Willison, who named prompt injection in 2022, gave the pattern a name in June 2025: the lethal trifecta.

21
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.291
Three capabilities.

22
00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:46.773
Access to private data.

23
00:01:46.823 --> 00:01:48.256
Exposure to untrusted content.

24
00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:54.926
And a way to communicate externally: send an email, call a web address, write to a shared place.

25
00:01:54.976 --> 00:02:06.784
An agent that has all three can be made to leak the private data, and no prompt will stop it, because the attack is written in the same language as your instructions.

26
00:02:06.834 --> 00:02:13.083
Remove any one of the three and the worst case shrinks to something you can live with.

27
00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.048
Copilot in the EchoLeak case had all three.

28
00:02:16.798 --> 00:02:20.114
Why can you not just prompt your way out?

29
00:02:20.164 --> 00:02:23.854
Because the attacker gets to iterate and you do not.

30
00:02:23.904 --> 00:02:29.090
Instructions to ignore injected text work until someone finds the phrasing that gets through.

31
00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.586
Filters catch yesterday's attacks.

32
00:02:30.636 --> 00:02:35.074
What works is architecture: do not give one agent all three legs.

33
00:02:35.124 --> 00:02:38.067
A common pattern is to split the work.

34
00:02:38.117 --> 00:02:44.051
One context reads the untrusted content and can only produce a summary or a structured extract.

35
00:02:44.101 --> 00:02:51.157
A different context, which never saw the raw content, decides what to do and holds the tools that act.

36
00:02:51.207 --> 00:02:57.141
The CaMeL design from Google DeepMind, published in 2025, is the careful version of this idea.

37
00:02:57.891 --> 00:03:01.318
In the first part of lab five, map your own agent.

38
00:03:01.368 --> 00:03:08.272
List every input it reads that you do not control: documents, emails, web pages, tool results from systems other people write to.

39
00:03:08.322 --> 00:03:11.117
List every tool that sends, writes or calls out.

40
00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:13.330
List the private data it can reach.

41
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:23.129
If all three lists have something in them, you have the trifecta, and the deliverable is to name the leg you will cut or gate, and show it in the trace.

42
00:03:23.179 --> 00:03:26.289
Next video: the gates and guardrails that do the cutting.
